WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.460
<v Maya>Welcome back to part two of our scheduling series.

2
00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:07.030
<v Maya>Last time we defined scheduling and met four objectives.

3
00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.180
<v Maya>Today we make two of them practical.

4
00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:12.880
<v Maya>How often can a muscle be trained, and why does a rest day matter.

5
00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:15.720
<v Maya>Then we start macro and micro programming.

6
00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:20.780
<v Dev>So today is numbers, days of the week, and a gym day built step by step.

7
00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:21.680
<v Maya>Yes.

8
00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:23.230
<v Maya>Let's start with a table.

9
00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:27.680
<v Maya>It links the number of sets you do for a muscle to the recovery that muscle needs.

10
00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:30.220
<v Maya>One to three sets need forty eight hours.

11
00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:32.930
<v Maya>Four to five sets need seventy two hours.

12
00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:35.590
<v Maya>More than five sets need about one week.

13
00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.500
<v Dev>More sets, more recovery.

14
00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:39.330
<v Dev>That is common sense.

15
00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:42.030
<v Maya>It is, and the table comes from research.

16
00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:43.820
<v Maya>Now an important detail.

17
00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:46.630
<v Maya>The sets are main sets per muscle, per session.

18
00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:48.210
<v Maya>Not per exercise.

19
00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:49.720
<v Maya>Say you train the back.

20
00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:56.990
<v Maya>Two main sets of lat pulldown plus one main set of a supported row equals three main sets for the latissimus dorsi.

21
00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:58.530
<v Maya>Three for the muscle.

22
00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:00.170
<v Dev>And warm-up sets?

23
00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:01.360
<v Maya>Not counted.

24
00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:03.010
<v Maya>Neither are prep sets.

25
00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:04.610
<v Maya>Only main sets.

26
00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:05.670
<v Dev>Okay.

27
00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:07.610
<v Dev>Now the weekly outlook.

28
00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:10.900
<v Maya>Imagine three people, call them Person A, B and C.

29
00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:12.490
<v Maya>Write Monday to Sunday.

30
00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:16.020
<v Maya>Person A does one to three sets and needs forty eight hours.

31
00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:17.340
<v Maya>Trains Monday.

32
00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:19.900
<v Maya>When can they train the muscle next, Dev?

33
00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:23.010
<v Dev>Forty eight hours is two days, so Wednesday?

34
00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:25.070
<v Maya>Yes, Wednesday, not Thursday.

35
00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:31.520
<v Maya>Tuesday is one full day, and the remaining hours of Monday plus the early hours of Wednesday make another day.

36
00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:37.540
<v Maya>Monday at two in the afternoon to Wednesday at two in the afternoon is exactly forty eight hours.

37
00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:39.850
<v Dev>So Monday, Wednesday, Friday.

38
00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:40.740
<v Maya>Right.

39
00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:47.040
<v Maya>After Friday, forty eight hours runs through Saturday into Sunday, and Sunday is the mandatory rest day.

40
00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:48.910
<v Maya>So the next session is Monday.

41
00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:51.280
<v Maya>Frequency, three times a week.

42
00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:52.620
<v Dev>Person B?

43
00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:55.290
<v Maya>Four to five sets, seventy two hours.

44
00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:56.980
<v Maya>Monday, then Thursday.

45
00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:02.820
<v Maya>After Thursday, seventy two hours would land on Sunday, which is rest, so the next one is Monday.

46
00:02:02.870 --> 00:02:04.480
<v Maya>Twice a week, not more.

47
00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:06.370
<v Maya>More would be overtraining.

48
00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:09.120
<v Dev>And Person C, more than five sets?

49
00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:10.560
<v Maya>Needs a full week.

50
00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:12.660
<v Maya>Monday, and again next Monday.

51
00:02:12.710 --> 00:02:14.010
<v Maya>Once a week.

52
00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:16.470
<v Dev>So the memory hook is three, two, one.

53
00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:18.800
<v Dev>One to three sets, three times.

54
00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:20.460
<v Dev>Four to five, twice.

55
00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:22.220
<v Dev>Over five, once.

56
00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:23.150
<v Maya>Lovely.

57
00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:27.950
<v Maya>And the once a week row matches the bro split, where one body part is trained per day.

58
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.020
<v Maya>Back day, chest day, and so on.

59
00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:38.570
<v Maya>The course says the later lectures discuss who should use which split, because the industry has stereotypes, like full body being only for beginners.

60
00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:40.570
<v Dev>Does this apply to everyone?

61
00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:43.930
<v Dev>What about people who use enhancing substances?

62
00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:45.160
<v Maya>Good question.

63
00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:49.370
<v Maya>Everything here assumes natural training, no artificial substances.

64
00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:53.490
<v Maya>Enhanced people have extra capacity and a different recovery pattern.

65
00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:56.790
<v Maya>The studies behind these numbers were all on natural training.

66
00:02:56.840 --> 00:02:58.310
<v Maya>Now, why a rest day.

67
00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:01.000
<v Maya>Not giving one is a way of overtraining.

68
00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:02.890
<v Maya>There are three recoveries.

69
00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:04.090
<v Dev>Let me guess.

70
00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:05.340
<v Dev>Muscles?

71
00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:07.800
<v Maya>Musculoskeletal recovery.

72
00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:13.810
<v Maya>That means soft tissues, tendons, ligaments, muscles and fascia, and hard tissues, the bones.

73
00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:18.590
<v Maya>People focus on the main muscles and forget the joints that are shared across days.

74
00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:20.230
<v Maya>Take the shoulder joint.

75
00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:22.930
<v Maya>It works on back day, chest day and shoulder day.

76
00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:26.330
<v Maya>The same tendons and ligaments work across days.

77
00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:29.950
<v Dev>But people say, I'll just play a cricket match on Sunday.

78
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.340
<v Maya>And the course answer is, you cannot leave your femur and humerus at home.

79
00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:36.430
<v Maya>You cannot leave your muscles at home.

80
00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:38.810
<v Maya>A full rest day is mandatory.

81
00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:40.460
<v Dev>Second recovery?

82
00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:44.730
<v Maya>CNS and PNS, the central and peripheral nervous systems.

83
00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:49.250
<v Maya>Think of the CNS as the factory where the contraction signals are produced.

84
00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:52.440
<v Maya>The PNS are the truck drivers who deliver them.

85
00:03:52.490 --> 00:03:55.810
<v Maya>They work twenty four hours a day, even in daily tasks.

86
00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:57.500
<v Maya>Both deserve a day off.

87
00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:02.270
<v Maya>The slides list only the CNS, but the lecture includes the PNS.

88
00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:04.440
<v Dev>And the third is immunity.

89
00:04:04.490 --> 00:04:05.330
<v Maya>Yes.

90
00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:08.190
<v Maya>Immunity is a shield, and stress wears it down.

91
00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:12.700
<v Maya>Some stress is non controllable, like financial or relationship stress.

92
00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:15.550
<v Maya>Some is controllable, and here the rest day helps.

93
00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:19.750
<v Maya>A full rest day helps keep cortisol, the stress hormone, in check.

94
00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:21.880
<v Dev>Factory, truck drivers, shield.

95
00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:23.650
<v Dev>I can hold those.

96
00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:24.600
<v Maya>Recap.

97
00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:26.550
<v Maya>Sets are main sets per muscle.

98
00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:30.060
<v Maya>One to three sets, forty eight hours, three times a week.

99
00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:32.640
<v Maya>Four to five, seventy two hours, twice.

100
00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:34.740
<v Maya>More than five, one week, once.

101
00:04:34.790 --> 00:04:37.560
<v Maya>Sunday is the rest day for three recoveries.

102
00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:39.270
<v Maya>Here is a pause and think.

103
00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:45.380
<v Maya>If someone does four main sets for their chest on Monday, when is the next chest session, and how often a week?

104
00:04:45.430 --> 00:04:48.910
<v Dev>Four sets is the seventy two hour row, so Thursday.

105
00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:52.180
<v Dev>And only twice a week, because Sunday is rest.

106
00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:53.170
<v Maya>Correct.

107
00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:55.250
<v Maya>Not Wednesday, and not three times.

108
00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:56.400
<v Maya>Next aisle.

109
00:04:56.450 --> 00:04:59.200
<v Maya>The two major types of exercise programming.

110
00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:00.830
<v Maya>Macro and micro.

111
00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:02.390
<v Dev>Big and small?

112
00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:03.530
<v Maya>Exactly.

113
00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:06.090
<v Maya>Macro is the high level, the bigger picture.

114
00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:09.300
<v Maya>Micro is the zoomed in picture, like the microscope.

115
00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:11.000
<v Maya>A student said it best.

116
00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:14.520
<v Maya>Macro is a forest, micro is a tree in that forest.

117
00:05:14.570 --> 00:05:16.300
<v Dev>Examples please.

118
00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:18.180
<v Maya>The certification itself.

119
00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:21.800
<v Maya>The macro programme is three modules, each with a unit test.

120
00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:29.990
<v Maya>The micro programme is a single lecture, with a recap like a warm-up, the main content like the main sets, and a quiz at the end like a cool-down.

121
00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:32.490
<v Maya>Another example is a family wedding.

122
00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:34.840
<v Maya>Macro, four functions in one week.

123
00:05:34.890 --> 00:05:39.660
<v Maya>Micro, the timings of one day, when the caterer serves, when the DJ starts.

124
00:05:39.710 --> 00:05:41.430
<v Dev>And for exercise?

125
00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:47.220
<v Maya>Macro is the client's whole fitness journey, with specific goals and the activities that meet them.

126
00:05:47.270 --> 00:05:49.350
<v Maya>It comes first, like a blueprint.

127
00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:53.620
<v Maya>If something is missing at macro level, it will be missing at micro level.

128
00:05:53.670 --> 00:05:55.910
<v Dev>So what's in the macro table?

129
00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:57.080
<v Maya>Four rows.

130
00:05:57.130 --> 00:06:03.300
<v Maya>Cardiorespiratory endurance and muscular endurance together are met by aerobic cross training.

131
00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:11.610
<v Maya>A diverse pool of aerobic activities improves cardiorespiratory endurance and builds comprehensive muscular endurance.

132
00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:17.600
<v Maya>Musculoskeletal strength is met by weight training, also called strength or resistance training.

133
00:06:17.650 --> 00:06:25.030
<v Maya>Ideal body composition is met by the hypertrophy tripod, progressive overload, nutrition, and rest and recovery.

134
00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:28.160
<v Maya>The ideal male body fat is under fifteen percent.

135
00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:30.850
<v Maya>And flexibility is met by stretching.

136
00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:34.010
<v Dev>Is the activity an exercise, like squats?

137
00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:34.850
<v Maya>No.

138
00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:39.040
<v Maya>It is the type of activity, not the number of sets or the exercise.

139
00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:43.100
<v Dev>Strength training, stretching, aerobic cross training, tripod.

140
00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:44.470
<v Dev>Four rows, fine.

141
00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:45.700
<v Maya>Now micro.

142
00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:47.200
<v Maya>Imagine a leg day.

143
00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:48.680
<v Maya>You walk into the gym.

144
00:06:48.730 --> 00:06:49.940
<v Maya>First thing?

145
00:06:49.990 --> 00:06:51.220
<v Dev>Warm up?

146
00:06:51.270 --> 00:06:57.310
<v Maya>Step one is the general warm-up, a slow walk of two to three minutes to wake the body and the CNS.

147
00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:02.240
<v Maya>Step two is the mobility drill, which is dynamic stretching, done before the workout.

148
00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:07.190
<v Maya>It stimulates the CNS, improves blood flow and removes joint stiffness.

149
00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:09.390
<v Dev>Some people merge those two.

150
00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:13.890
<v Maya>That is acceptable, but micro programming is detailed, so keep them separate.

151
00:07:13.940 --> 00:07:17.230
<v Maya>Then steps three, four and five are the three types of sets.

152
00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:20.390
<v Maya>Specific warm-up set, prep sets, and main sets.

153
00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:22.520
<v Dev>Why is it called specific?

154
00:07:22.570 --> 00:07:25.590
<v Maya>Specific to that exercise, not just the muscle.

155
00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:31.180
<v Maya>If the main set is a squat, the warm-up set is also a squat, with body weight or an empty bar.

156
00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:34.950
<v Maya>You check the exact movement pattern is pain free before loading.

157
00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:38.670
<v Maya>Warming up squats with leg extensions is not specific.

158
00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:40.140
<v Dev>And prep sets?

159
00:07:40.190 --> 00:07:43.960
<v Maya>They gradually increase the weight to prepare soft and hard tissues.

160
00:07:44.010 --> 00:07:47.640
<v Maya>For example forty, sixty and eighty percent of one RM.

161
00:07:47.690 --> 00:07:49.410
<v Maya>There is no rule of three.

162
00:07:49.460 --> 00:07:50.490
<v Maya>It varies.

163
00:07:50.540 --> 00:07:54.130
<v Maya>Then the main sets, the final battle, with four to six RM.

164
00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:57.510
<v Maya>Step six is static passive stretching, at the end.

165
00:07:57.560 --> 00:07:59.080
<v Dev>Why at the end?

166
00:07:59.130 --> 00:08:06.770
<v Maya>A static stretch is held for about twenty five to thirty seconds, enough for the stretch reflex followed by autogenic inhibition.

167
00:08:06.820 --> 00:08:08.580
<v Maya>Then the muscle relaxes.

168
00:08:08.630 --> 00:08:12.370
<v Maya>If it is relaxed at the start, it cannot produce force in your sets.

169
00:08:12.420 --> 00:08:15.240
<v Maya>The slides say thirty seconds, so use thirty.

170
00:08:15.290 --> 00:08:21.680
<v Maya>The lecture once also mentioned fifteen seconds as the minimum for the stretch mechanism, so check your material.

171
00:08:21.730 --> 00:08:24.170
<v Dev>Do beginners use all those sets?

172
00:08:24.220 --> 00:08:25.010
<v Maya>No.

173
00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:27.160
<v Maya>Beginners use only learning sets.

174
00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:31.740
<v Maya>Intermediates use a specific warm-up set and main sets, with no prep set.

175
00:08:31.790 --> 00:08:33.430
<v Maya>Advanced use all three.

176
00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:38.300
<v Maya>And the three minute rest only applies to main sets, so beginners never use it.

177
00:08:38.350 --> 00:08:43.020
<v Dev>And someone who is in the gym every day, do they need a specific warm-up?

178
00:08:43.070 --> 00:08:43.910
<v Maya>Yes.

179
00:08:43.960 --> 00:08:48.800
<v Maya>They may be overtraining, and overnight the muscle may react differently to that movement.

180
00:08:48.850 --> 00:08:56.330
<v Maya>But it is needed for the main compound lifts, not for a biceps curl after pulling exercises, because those muscles already worked.

181
00:08:56.380 --> 00:08:58.000
<v Maya>Five things to remember.

182
00:08:58.050 --> 00:09:05.470
<v Maya>One, main sets per muscle: one to three sets need forty eight hours, four to five need seventy two, more than five need a week.

183
00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:08.210
<v Dev>Two, that gives three, two and once a week.

184
00:09:08.260 --> 00:09:11.010
<v Dev>Sunday is the mandatory rest day.

185
00:09:11.060 --> 00:09:16.910
<v Maya>Three, the rest day gives musculoskeletal, nervous system, and immune system recovery.

186
00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:20.080
<v Dev>Four, macro is the forest, the whole journey.

187
00:09:20.130 --> 00:09:22.560
<v Dev>Micro is the tree, one session.

188
00:09:22.610 --> 00:09:25.950
<v Dev>The macro table pairs each goal with an activity.

189
00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:28.590
<v Maya>Five, the weight training day is six steps.

190
00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:35.390
<v Maya>General warm-up, mobility drill, specific warm-up set, prep sets, main sets, static passive stretching last.

191
00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:40.510
<v Maya>Next time, the cardio day and the combined day, and then the fundamentals and the four pillars.

